00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:53.840
So, with NERFs, we are able to gather a bunch of&nbsp;
photos, and have a technique stitch it together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:00.120
into a virtual world. There are models that&nbsp;
can do this extremely quickly, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:06.960
Instant Neural Graphics. This converges in a&nbsp;
matter of seconds, which is kind of insane.&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:14.400
And its quality is often even better than its&nbsp;
predecessors. You see a great deal more detail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:22.400
in the hair and the sweater. And now, let’s&nbsp;
see the new technique! Ready to be blown away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:29.600
Wait a second… this looks nearly the same!&nbsp;
So is this better? If it is better, how?

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:37.320
Well, what you see here is quality, but quality&nbsp;
is just half of the story! The other half is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.560
size. We haven’t talked about that yet.&nbsp;
The first technique is reasonably sized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:49.080
but the quality is lacking, then comes&nbsp;
Instant Neural Graphics, quality much better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:56.280
but the size is much larger. And now, look at&nbsp;
the new technique, which looks roughly the same,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:05.760
but, oh my, it packs the same quality but in one&nbsp;
fifth the size. Fantastic. In this sense, this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:11.680
even better than the legendary new technique,&nbsp;
Gaussian Splatting, which can create and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:19.560
even animate virtual worlds, and this new one&nbsp;
is 50 times more compact than that. Crazy.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:26.560
Now, let’s sculpt some images. Second paper.&nbsp;
Here, the goal is to take an image, any image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:33.680
and then, convert the people or objects in&nbsp;
it into a 3D model, but not to create a video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:40.160
game character from them, although that’s quite&nbsp;
nice too, but no, not here! Here, we now have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:47.160
knowledge about the backside of this model too,&nbsp;
so we can choose a new pose for our character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:54.960
and apply some more magic to put it back into the&nbsp;
image with the new pose. We can even rotate them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:01.360
you name it. Shifting these objects to&nbsp;
new positions is also possible. And wait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:08.480
these are 3D models, so we can even apply&nbsp;
deformations to them. Carve out that bad boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:14.040
and there we go! Apart from some&nbsp;
suspect artifacts at the mouth region,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:19.920
this one is almost perfect. Or placing&nbsp;
new ducklings or fish into an image?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:26.280
Not a problem. And this concept gives us a great&nbsp;
deal of control over these images. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:32.720
how many cherries would you like? How about&nbsp;
this one? And another one? And another one? The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:40.480
consistency between the images is not perfect, but&nbsp;
they are nearly the same. And just imagine what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:47.760
we will be capable of just two more papers down&nbsp;
the line. My goodness. What a time to be alive!

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:53.120
And, we are not done with magic for today,&nbsp;
not even close. With this other work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:59.960
we can apply some more artistic direction to&nbsp;
already existing images. Just look at the arrows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:07.440
this indicates our wishes as to how the image&nbsp;
should be moving, and bam! We get a video. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:14.200
works great for camera movement, but you know&nbsp;
what. I wonder what happens if I instruct this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:22.040
horse to move. That is so much more complex than&nbsp;
just camera movement. So, what happens then? Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:29.680
hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars, and…my&nbsp;
goodness. Look at that. The AI understands how a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:37.720
horse should move, and synthesizes exactly that.&nbsp;
It is not perfect, not even close, but this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:44.720
once again an excellent opportunity to invoke&nbsp;
the First Law of Papers. What is that? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:50.480
the First Law of Papers says that research&nbsp;
is a process. Do not look at where we are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:56.520
look at where we will be two more papers down&nbsp;
the line. Remember what DALL-E 1 could do in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:03.440
terms of text to image, and then, DALL-E 2&nbsp;
dropped and blew it out of the water. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:09.480
imagine what a DALL-E 2 moment for this&nbsp;
